<mujoco model="ground">
    <!--
      Ramp terrain for the rolling-contact work: flat ground with four driveable
      ramp lanes, used to separate "constant slope" from "slope transition".

      WHY THIS IS A `ground` MODEL AND NOT PART OF THE ROBOT
      mc_mujoco builds its scene by merging one MuJoCo model per mc_rtc robot
      (mj_utils_merge_mujoco_models.cpp), resolving `<name>.yaml` from its user
      folder first and its share folder second. `mc_control::MCController` always
      loads `env/ground` as a second robot (src/mc_control/MCController.cpp:96),
      so `ground.yaml` is the one hook that puts geometry in the world without
      touching the robot description or the controller. Every name below is
      prefixed with `ground_` in the merged scene, exactly as the stock
      `env/ground.xml` is, so `ground_base_link` and `ground_floor` keep their
      meaning for anything that looks them up by name.

      `<include>` is NOT an option here: mc_mujoco expands MuJoCo includes only
      inside `asset`, `contact`, `actuator` and `sensor`
      (mj_utils_merge_mujoco_models.cpp:344-363), never inside `worldbody`, so a
      "scene that includes the robot" cannot be assembled that way.

      LAYOUT (all lanes run along +x; the robot drives up, across and down)
        y =  -4.0   flat reference, no geometry at all
        y =   0.0   5 deg
        y =   4.0   10 deg
        y =   8.0   15 deg
        y =  12.0   20 deg
      Each lane is 2.4 m wide and has the same longitudinal stations:
        x = 0.600   toe    (flat -> incline; the surface tangent steps 0 -> theta)
        x = 2.100   crest  (incline -> plateau)
        x = 3.100   plateau end (plateau -> descent)
        x = 4.600   toe-out (descent -> flat)
      so a lane's crest height is 1.5 * tan(theta).

      GEOMETRY CONSTRUCTION
      Each incline is a single box of half-sizes (L/(2 cos t), 1.2, 0.5) rotated
      about +y by -t (quat = [cos(t/2), 0, -sin(t/2), 0]) and placed so that its
      top face contains the segment from (x_toe, 0) to (x_crest, h):
        centre = (x_toe + L/2 + 0.5 sin t,  y_lane,  h/2 - 0.5 cos t)
      The 0.5 m half-thickness buries the slab under the floor plane over the
      whole lane, including the 20 deg one. Floor and ramps share the single
      `base_link` body, so MuJoCo generates no contacts between them and the
      buried parts are inert.

      The plateau overlaps its two neighbours by 1 mm on each side so that no
      hairline gap can open at the crest from decimal rounding; the resulting
      lip is at most 1 mm * tan(20 deg) = 0.36 mm, three orders of magnitude
      below the 0.125 m wheel radius.

      THE POINT OF THE TOE
      Standing assumption A4 of the formulation (`as:one-plane` in
      rolling-contact-qp.tex) is that all four contacts share one plane, and the
      controller takes `terrainNormal` as a configuration constant. On the flat
      and on the plateau that holds. Between x = 0.6 - 0.494 and x = 0.6 the
      front and rear axles are on surfaces with different normals and it does
      not. That interval is the object of study, not a defect of this model.
    -->

    <visual>
      <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6"  ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" specular="0 0 0"/>
      <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
      <!--
        mc_mujoco merges each <visual> child individually (never <statistic>,
        which the merger drops silently), so this is the one place that can
        fix contact-point/contact-force marker sizing for the ramps scene
        without touching the robot model.

        `scale.*` renders relative to mjModel.stat.meansize (the header's
        "relative to mean body size", NOT stat.extent, despite the name
        `scale/contactwidth` suggesting otherwise); `map/force` is a
        newton-to-metre length factor divided by stat.meanmass, independent
        of meansize/extent. Confirmed empirically both on an isolated probe
        body and, since that first pass missed the meanmass division (the
        probe's mass was 1 kg, masking it), by directly reading back
        mjv_updateScene's mjvGeom sizes for this merged ramps scene's own
        settled wheel contacts:
          contact-point radius      = scale.contactwidth  * stat.meansize
          contact-point half-height = scale.contactheight * stat.meansize
          force-arrow shaft radius  = scale.forcewidth    * stat.meansize
          force-arrow length        = normal_force[N] * map.force / stat.meanmass

        This merged scene's stat.meansize is ~1.54 and stat.meanmass is 7.5
        (the ramp terrain is far bigger than the vehicle), so the MuJoCo
        defaults (contactwidth=0.3, contactheight=forcewidth=0.1,
        map/force=0.005) render a 0.46 m-radius, 0.31 m-tall contact disc on
        a vehicle whose wheels are 0.125 m in radius and 0.08 m wide: the
        disc alone is bigger than the 0.68 m chassis. The force arrow itself
        is comparatively short at this scale (measured ~0.11-0.14 m at the
        ~157-210 N per-wheel settled load), but that is *shorter* than the
        disc's own 0.15 m half-height, so the arrow's entire length, base to
        tip, fits inside the disc's volume -- which is why forces looked
        altogether invisible rather than merely oversized.

        Values below are sized against the robot instead: at this
        meansize, contactwidth=0.012/contactheight=0.004 give a ~1.8 cm
        radius / ~0.6 cm half-height contact marker (legible against the
        0.08 m wheel width without covering it); forcewidth=0.004 gives a
        ~0.6 cm shaft (thinner than the point marker, so the arrow reads as
        distinct from it); map/force=0.0025 gives a ~6 cm arrow at the
        ~184 N/wheel steady-state load -- comfortably longer than the point
        marker so it is no longer swallowed by it, and still well under the
        0.25 m wheel diameter and 0.68 m chassis length even at several
        times that load. Because the arrow points from the ground contact
        straight up into the wheel, and any length under the 0.25 m wheel
        diameter stays inside the wheel's own circular cross-section, it
        remains partly self-occluded by the (opaque) wheel from most
        outside viewpoints regardless of map/force -- confirmed with a
        transparency-forced diagnostic render showing the arrow present and
        correctly placed. That occlusion is inherent to visualising a
        near-vertical contact force at a wheeled contact, not a defect this
        file can fix by choosing a different map/force.
      -->
      <scale contactwidth="0.012" contactheight="0.004" forcewidth="0.004"/>
      <map force="0.0025"/>
    </visual>

    <asset>
        <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7" rgb2="0 0 0" width="512" height="3072"/>

        <texture name="texplane" type="2d" builtin="checker" rgb1=".2 .3 .4" rgb2=".1 0.15 0.2" width="512" height="512" mark="cross" markrgb=".8 .8 .8"/>
        <material name="matplane" reflectance="0.3" texture="texplane" texrepeat="1 1" texuniform="true"/>
        <material name="ramp_05" rgba="0.20 0.55 0.30 1"/>
        <material name="ramp_10" rgba="0.55 0.55 0.20 1"/>
        <material name="ramp_15" rgba="0.65 0.40 0.15 1"/>
        <material name="ramp_20" rgba="0.65 0.20 0.20 1"/>
    </asset>

    <worldbody>
      <light pos="0 0 2" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>
      <body name="base_link">
        <geom name="floor" pos="0 0 0" size="0 0 .25" type="plane" material="matplane" condim="3" group="1"/>

        <!-- 5 deg lane, centre y = 0.0, crest height 0.1312 m -->
        <geom name="ramp_05_up" type="box" material="ramp_05" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.752865 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="1.393578 0.000000 -0.432481" quat="0.999048 0 -0.043619 0"/>
        <geom name="ramp_05_top" type="box" material="ramp_05" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.501000 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="2.600000 0.000000 -0.368767"/>
        <geom name="ramp_05_down" type="box" material="ramp_05" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.752865 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="3.806422 0.000000 -0.432481" quat="0.999048 0 0.043619 0"/>

        <!-- 10 deg lane, centre y = 4.0, crest height 0.2645 m -->
        <geom name="ramp_10_up" type="box" material="ramp_10" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.761570 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="1.436824 4.000000 -0.360159" quat="0.996195 0 -0.087156 0"/>
        <geom name="ramp_10_top" type="box" material="ramp_10" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.501000 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="2.600000 4.000000 -0.235510"/>
        <geom name="ramp_10_down" type="box" material="ramp_10" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.761570 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="3.763176 4.000000 -0.360159" quat="0.996195 0 0.087156 0"/>

        <!-- 15 deg lane, centre y = 8.0, crest height 0.4019 m -->
        <geom name="ramp_15_up" type="box" material="ramp_15" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.776457 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="1.479410 8.000000 -0.282001" quat="0.991445 0 -0.130526 0"/>
        <geom name="ramp_15_top" type="box" material="ramp_15" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.501000 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="2.600000 8.000000 -0.098076"/>
        <geom name="ramp_15_down" type="box" material="ramp_15" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.776457 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="3.720590 8.000000 -0.282001" quat="0.991445 0 0.130526 0"/>

        <!-- 20 deg lane, centre y = 12.0, crest height 0.5460 m -->
        <geom name="ramp_20_up" type="box" material="ramp_20" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.798133 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="1.521010 12.000000 -0.196869" quat="0.984808 0 -0.173648 0"/>
        <geom name="ramp_20_top" type="box" material="ramp_20" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.501000 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="2.600000 12.000000 0.045955"/>
        <geom name="ramp_20_down" type="box" material="ramp_20" condim="3" group="1"
              size="0.798133 1.200000 0.500000"
              pos="3.678990 12.000000 -0.196869" quat="0.984808 0 0.173648 0"/>
      </body>
    </worldbody>
</mujoco>
